---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL, LAST_UPDATED, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
const lang = 'en';
---
<PageLayout title={`Editorial policy of ${SITE_NAMES[lang]}: sources and corrections`} description="Which BACB handbooks and state board pages Behavior Tech Exam relies on, how its practice questions are written, which sources it refuses and how a mistake is corrected and dated." lang={lang} noindex={true} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Editorial policy' }]} showAuthor={false}>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Editorial and corrections policy</h1>
    <p>A behavior technician candidate pays fees, schedules an appointment and often has a job offer that depends on passing within a set time. A wrong deadline or a wrong answer costs money and weeks, so every figure here points to the document it was read in, and every question to the outline task it trains.</p>
    <h2>Who is responsible</h2><p>{AUTHOR_NAME}, reachable at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
    <h2>What we read</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>the certification board's documents: the RBT Handbook, the RBT Test Content Outline (3rd ed.), the 40-hour training requirements, the initial and renewal competency assessment packets, the pass rate report, the handbook and outline of the analyst exam (BCBA, Board Certified Behavior Analyst);</li>
      <li>the scheduling page of the test vendor;</li>
      <li>state laws, rules and board pages for the states that credential technicians (Washington, Oregon, Nevada, Louisiana) and the board pages of other states we cite;</li>
      <li>the board's own compiled list and brief of state licensure laws, labelled as such.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>How the questions are written</h2><p>Each question takes one task of the outline and builds a short scenario of our own, with four options, one right answer and an explanation. We do not copy questions from prep companies, books, flash-card sites or the real exam, and we never claim a question is from the real exam.</p>
    <h2>Sources we refuse</h2><p>Training companies, prep sites, forums and salary aggregators never supply a figure here. When an official page could not be read (some state pages refuse automated reading), the site names the page it used instead and says so.</p>
    <h2>Clinical content</h2><p>The scenarios describe what the exam expects from a technician working under a supervisor. They are not treatment advice, and a behavior plan is always written and adjusted by the supervising analyst.</p>
    <h2>Review cycle</h2><p>Fees and rules are read again when the board publishes a new handbook or outline, and at least every three months. Changes announced for a later date are shown with that date, never as current.</p>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <ol><li>We read the source again.</li><li>We correct the parameter file or the question and add a test for the case.</li><li>The site rebuilds and every page shows the new date.</li></ol>
    <h2>Independence</h2><p>No board, vendor, provider or employer pays for or reviews what is published, and there is no affiliate link. See the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a>. Version of <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-US')}</time>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
